WN.lb(21,{"mt_f9pqp4Tjny":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which is smaller: a mouse or a horse?","choices":["Mouse","Horse","They are the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one could fit in your hand?","explain":"A mouse is small and takes up little room. A horse is big."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Two towers stand on the floor. One has {n} blocks and one has 1 block. Which is shorter?","choices":["The one with 1 block","The one with more blocks","They are the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Both start on the floor. Which one stays low?","explain":"Both start on the floor, so the 1-block tower stays lower. It is shorter than the {n}-block tower.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A short tower is on a chair. A tall tower is on the floor. Which tower is taller?","choices":["The one on the floor","The one on the chair","They are the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"The chair lifts a tower up. It does not add blocks.","explain":"The chair only lifts the short tower up high. Put both on the floor and the floor tower is taller."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"To see which of two crayons is longer, what do you do first?","choices":["Line up the ends","Pick the one that sticks out","Pick your best colour"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is only fair when both start at the same place.","explain":"Line up the starts. Then the one that goes further at the other end is longer."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A train has {n} carriages. Another train has 1 carriage. Which train is longer?","choices":["The one with more carriages","The one with 1 carriage","They are the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each carriage makes a train go further along the track.","explain":"The train with {n} carriages goes further along, so it is longer than the train with 1.","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which word fits a giraffe?","choices":["Tall","Long","Small"],"answer":0,"hint":"A giraffe stands up, and its head is way up high.","explain":"A giraffe stands up high, so tall is the best word. Long is for things that lie along."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Three bowls: small, middle and big. Which one goes last in a small-to-big line?","choices":["The big one","The small one","The middle one"],"answer":0,"hint":"The line ends with the one that takes up most room.","explain":"Small to big ends with the biggest bowl: small, then middle, then big."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which is bigger: a bus or a ball?","choices":["Bus","Ball","They are the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one takes up lots of room on the road?","explain":"A bus is big. It takes up much more room than a ball, which is small."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Your tower has {n} blocks. Bear's tower has 2 blocks. Whose tower is taller?","choices":["Mine","Bear's","They are the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"More blocks stacked up make a tower go higher.","explain":"Your {n} blocks go up higher than Bear's 2 blocks, so your tower is taller.","params":{"n":[3,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A worm goes on and on along the ground. Which word fits?","choices":["Long","Tall","Heavy"],"answer":0,"hint":"Tall is for things that stand up. What word is for things that lie along?","explain":"A worm lies along the ground, so we say it is long. Tall is for things that go up high."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Tom's tower has {n} blocks. Sam's tower has {n} blocks too, but it stands on a box. Whose tower is taller?","choices":["They are the same","Sam's, it is on a box","Tom's"],"answer":0,"hint":"The box lifts Sam's tower up. Does it add any blocks?","explain":"Both towers have {n} blocks, so they are the same height. The box only lifts Sam's tower up high.","params":{"n":[2,6]}}],"explain":{"kid":["An elephant is big. A mouse is small. Big things take up lots of room. Small things take up a bit of room. A giraffe is tall. It goes up high. A cat is short. Tall is for things that stand up.","A snake can be long. A long thing goes on and on. To see which crayon is long, put the ends side by side at a line. Then look at the far end.","A box can trick you. A short tower on a box looks tall. Put both towers on the floor, then look.","Line up three bears. Start with the small one. Then the middle one. Then the big one."],"adult":["At two and three, children learn to compare two things side by side and say how they differ: big and small for size, tall and short for height, long and short for length. Then they put three things in order by size, which is the start of measuring. The exact word matters. Tall is for height when something stands up. Long is for length along the ground. Big is the general word for size.","The lab gives each idea its own action. Children tap the big or small one of a pair, add and remove blocks to make a tower taller and then shorter than Bear's, slide a crayon back to the start line before choosing the longer one, move a tower off a box before comparing, and put three bears in order from smallest to biggest.","Common mistakes to watch for: judging length by which end sticks out further when the starts are not lined up; judging height by whose top is higher when one thing stands on a box or step; using big for every comparison instead of tall or long; and, when ordering three, finding the biggest and smallest but not checking the middle one. Correct these by lining things up together, not by telling.","Keep the language comparative and concrete: this one is longer, that one is shorter. Children of this age compare by looking side by side; numbers and rulers come later, so counting blocks here is only a check."]},"familyTip":"At bath or snack time, hold up two things, such as two spoons or two socks, and ask, \"Which is longer?\" Put their ends together on the table first. Then line up three cups or teddies from small to big and let your child check by lining up the bottoms.","keyIdea":"Big things take up lots of room and small things take up a little. Tall things go up high, and long things go a long way along. To compare fairly, start both things at the same line, then look at the other end. Put three things in order by starting with the smallest.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Big or small? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🐘 Big","🐭 Small"],"items":[["bus",0],["house",0],["whale",0],["tree",0],["ant",1],["pea",1],["bee",1],["button",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsize","caption":"Flagship lab: tap big and small, build a tower taller then shorter, line up crayon ends, take a tower off its box, and order three bears.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_fjf_vS04hs":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Water comes right up to the top of the cup. The cup is…","choices":["Full","Empty","Half full"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at where the water stops.","explain":"Water right to the top means the cup is full."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"One side of the scale goes up. The thing on that side is…","choices":["Lighter","Heavier","Bigger"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which side does the heavy thing pull?","explain":"The heavy thing pulls its side down, so the side that goes up has the lighter thing."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A teddy balances {n} blocks on the scale. How many blocks is the teddy as heavy as?","answer":"n","hint":"When the scale is level, both sides are just as heavy.","explain":"The scale is level with {n} blocks, so the teddy is as heavy as {n} blocks.","params":{"n":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A cup has a little water at the bottom. Is it empty?","choices":["No, there is still some water","Yes, it is empty","Yes, it is full"],"answer":0,"hint":"Empty means no water at all. Is there any left?","explain":"A little water is still water, so the cup is not empty. Empty means nothing inside."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which is lighter to hold: a leaf or a pumpkin?","choices":["The leaf","The pumpkin","You cannot tell"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one would pull your hand down?","explain":"The pumpkin pulls your hand down. The leaf hardly does, so the leaf is lighter."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"The water stops at the middle of the jar. The jar is…","choices":["Half full","Full","Empty"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it at the top, at the middle, or gone?","explain":"Water up to the middle means half full."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A big empty box and a small hammer. Which pulls the scale down?","choices":["The small hammer","The big empty box","Neither"],"answer":0,"hint":"Big is not the same as heavy.","explain":"The hammer is small but heavy. The empty box is big but light, so the hammer side goes down."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"The bucket is full after {n} scoops of sand. How many scoops do you pour in to make it full?","answer":"n","hint":"Full means the sand reaches the top. Count the scoops until it does.","explain":"It takes {n} scoops for the sand to reach the top, so {n} scoops make the bucket full.","params":{"n":[3,6]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"On the scale the book side goes down and the feather side goes up. Which is heavy and which is light?","choices":["The book is heavy, the feather is light","The feather is heavy, the book is light","They are just as heavy"],"answer":0,"hint":"The heavy side pulls the scale down.","explain":"The book side went down, so the book is heavy. The feather side went up, so the feather is light."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"The apple balances {n} blocks. The scale is level. How many blocks is the apple as heavy as?","answer":"n","hint":"Level means both sides are just as heavy.","explain":"The scale is level, so the apple is as heavy as the {n} blocks.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which is heavier: a big balloon or a small stone?","choices":["The small stone","The big balloon","They are the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Big is not the same as heavy. Think about holding them.","explain":"The balloon is big but full of air. The small stone pulls your hand down more, so it is heavier."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A full jug holds {n} cups of water. You pour out all {n} cups. How many cups of water are left in the jug?","answer":"n-n","hint":"You poured out every cup. What is left?","explain":"All {n} cups came out, so 0 are left. The jug is empty.","params":{"n":[2,5]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Hold a book in one hand and a feather in the other. The book pulls your hand down. The book is heavy. The feather is light.","A scale is like two hands. Put a thing on each side. The heavy side goes down. The light side goes up.","Big is not the same as heavy. A big balloon is full of air, so it is light. A small stone is heavy. Feel it or use the scale to find out.","Pour water in a cup. When it reaches the top, the cup is full. Pour it all out and the cup is empty. Water up to the middle means half full."],"adult":["This is the start of measures in the early years. Development Matters for 3 and 4 year olds asks children to make comparisons between objects relating to size, length, weight and capacity. In Year 1 the National Curriculum names the words: mass or weight (heavy, light, heavier than, lighter than) and capacity (full, empty, more than, less than, half full). US Common Core K.MD.A.2 asks children to compare two objects and say which has more of a measurable attribute.","At this age children compare directly, with no units: hefting one object in each hand, then using a simple balance, where the heavier pan goes down. Capacity is learned through water and sand play, filling and emptying containers and using full, empty and half full. Measuring with cubes or cups comes later.","Common mistakes: believing the bigger object must be heavier (size and mass are confused), reading the balance the wrong way so that the side that rises is called heavier, calling a cup full when it is nearly full, and calling a cup empty when a little is left. Ask 'how do you know?' so the child points to the scale or the top of the cup.","In the lab your child drags things onto a balance and picks the heavier or lighter one, tests big light things against small heavy ones, pours water to full, empty and half full against a halfway line, and names how full a jar, bowl or cup is."]},"familyTip":"At bath time give your child two cups and ask for one full and one empty, then one half full. At the shops let them hold two bags and say which is heavier, then check together with the scales.","keyIdea":"Hold one thing in each hand: the heavy one pulls your hand down. On a balance scale the heavier side goes down and the lighter side goes up. Big things are not always heavy: a big balloon is lighter than a small stone. A cup is full when the water reaches the top, empty when there is none left, and half full when the water stops at the middle.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Heavy or light? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🧱 Heavy","🪶 Light"],"items":[["brick",0],["pumpkin",0],["big bag of potatoes",0],["tin of beans",0],["feather",1],["leaf",1],["balloon",1],["tissue",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagheavy","caption":"Flagship lab: find the heavier and lighter thing on a balance, test big light things, and pour a cup full, empty and half full.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_WtcFrxGOgw":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A square has sides of {s} cm. What is its perimeter in cm?","answer":"4*s","hint":"A square has 4 equal sides. Add {s} four times.","explain":"{s} + {s} + {s} + {s} = {=4*s}, so the perimeter is {=4*s} cm.","params":{"s":[3,15]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A triangle has a perimeter of {=a+b+c} cm. Two sides are {a} cm and {b} cm. How long is the third side in cm?","answer":"c","hint":"Add the two sides you know, then take that from {=a+b+c}.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b}, and {=a+b+c} − {=a+b} = {c}. The third side is {c} cm.","params":{"a":[3,9],"b":[3,9],"c":[3,9]},"where":"c<a+b && a<b+c && b<a+c"},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A rectangle is 9 cm by 2 cm. Mo says the perimeter is 18 cm. What went wrong?","choices":["He found the area, 9 × 2, not the edge","Nothing, 18 cm is right","He added only two sides"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is 9 × 2 the distance round the edge or the squares inside?","explain":"9 × 2 = 18 is the area in squares. The perimeter is 9 + 2 + 9 + 2 = 22 cm."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A rectangle has a perimeter of 30 cm. One side is 10 cm. How long is the other side?","choices":["5 cm","20 cm","10 cm"],"answer":0,"hint":"There are two sides of 10 cm. Take both off, then halve what is left.","explain":"30 − 10 − 10 = 10 cm for the two other sides, so each is 5 cm. 20 cm takes off only one side, and 10 cm forgets to halve."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"12 squares are laid out as a 12 by 1 strip and as a 4 by 3 rectangle. Which has the longer perimeter?","choices":["The 12 by 1 strip","The 4 by 3 rectangle","They are the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Add the four sides of each one.","explain":"12 + 1 + 12 + 1 = 26 cm and 4 + 3 + 4 + 3 = 14 cm. Same area, but the strip has the longer perimeter."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"An octagon has 8 equal sides of 3 cm. What is its perimeter?","choices":["24 cm","11 cm","18 cm"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the sides: 8 of them, each 3 cm.","explain":"8 × 3 = 24, so the perimeter is 24 cm. 11 cm adds 8 and 3, and 18 cm counts only 6 sides."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A quadrilateral has a perimeter of 26 cm. Three sides are 9 cm, 4 cm and 7 cm. How long is the fourth side?","choices":["6 cm","20 cm","46 cm"],"answer":0,"hint":"Add the three sides you know, then subtract from 26.","explain":"9 + 4 + 7 = 20, and 26 − 20 = 6 cm. 20 cm is the known sides, and 46 cm adds the perimeter on."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which unit fits the perimeter of a playground?","choices":["metres (m)","square metres (m²)","kilograms (kg)"],"answer":0,"hint":"Perimeter is a length: how far round the edge.","explain":"Perimeter is a distance, so it is measured in metres. Square metres measure area, and kilograms measure mass."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"A rectangle is {a} cm long and {b} cm wide. What is its perimeter in cm?","answer":"2*a+2*b","hint":"Add all four sides: {a} + {b} + {a} + {b}.","explain":"{a} + {b} + {a} + {b} = {=2*a+2*b}, so the perimeter is {=2*a+2*b} cm.","params":{"a":[4,12],"b":[2,9]},"where":"a>b"},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A rectangle has a perimeter of {=2*a+2*b} cm. One side is {a} cm. How long is the other side in cm?","answer":"b","hint":"Take off both {a} cm sides, then halve what is left.","explain":"{=2*a+2*b} − {a} − {a} = {=2*b}, and half of {=2*b} is {b}. The other side is {b} cm.","params":{"a":[5,12],"b":[2,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A pentagon has sides of 6 cm, 4 cm, 5 cm, 7 cm and 3 cm. What is its perimeter?","choices":["25 cm","22 cm","5 cm"],"answer":0,"hint":"A pentagon has 5 sides. Tick each one as you add it.","explain":"6 + 4 + 5 + 7 + 3 = 25, so the perimeter is 25 cm. 22 cm misses the 3 cm side, and 5 cm is just the number of sides."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A 3 by 7 rectangle and a 5 by 5 square both have a perimeter of 20 cm. Which has the bigger area?","choices":["The 5 by 5 square","The 3 by 7 rectangle","They have the same area"],"answer":0,"hint":"Area is the squares inside: multiply length by width for each one.","explain":"3 + 7 + 3 + 7 = 20 and 5 + 5 + 5 + 5 = 20, so the perimeters match. But 5 × 5 = 25 squares and 3 × 7 = 21 squares, so the square has the bigger area."}],"explain":{"kid":["Put your finger on one corner of a shape. Run it along every side until you get back to the start. The distance your finger went is the perimeter. For a rectangle 8 cm long and 5 cm wide, that is 8 + 5 + 8 + 5 = 26 cm.","A polygon is a flat shape with straight sides. To find its perimeter, add up all its sides. Count the sides first, so you do not miss one. A hexagon has 6 sides. If each one is 4 cm, the perimeter is 6 × 4 = 24 cm.","Sometimes one side is missing. A pentagon has a perimeter of 30 cm, and four of its sides add up to 26 cm. The last side must be 30 − 26 = 4 cm. For a rectangle with perimeter 24 cm and one side of 8 cm, take off both 8 cm sides. That leaves 8 cm for the two other sides, so each is 4 cm.","Perimeter is not the same as area. Area counts the squares inside. A 6 by 6 square and an 11 by 1 strip both have a perimeter of 24 cm. But the square holds 36 squares and the strip holds only 11."],"adult":["This is US Common Core 3.MD.D.8. Children solve problems with the perimeters of polygons. They find the perimeter from side lengths, find an unknown side, and show rectangles with the same perimeter but different areas, or the same area but different perimeters. In England, Year 3 pupils measure the perimeter of simple 2-D shapes. Year 4 pupils measure and work out the perimeter of a rectilinear figure, including squares, in cm and m.","Perimeter is a length, so it is measured in cm or m. Area is measured in square units. For a rectangle, perimeter = 2 × (length + width). Let children add all four sides first, so the formula means something. Finding a missing side is the inverse: take the known sides from the perimeter. For a rectangle, take off both known sides and then halve what is left.","Common mistakes: counting the squares inside, which gives the area; adding only the two labelled sides of a rectangle; taking a known side off only once when finding a missing side; forgetting to halve at the end; adding the perimeter to the known sides; and miscounting the sides of a polygon. A careful check is to walk round the shape and tick each side as it is added.","In the lab your child walks round an 8 cm by 5 cm rectangle side by side, finds missing sides of a pentagon, a quadrilateral and a regular hexagon, solves rectangles from their perimeter, drags the corner of a rectangle that keeps a perimeter of 24 cm while its area changes, and builds every rectangle from 12 squares to compare their perimeters."]},"familyTip":"Use a piece of string to go round a book, a table mat or a door, then measure the string with a ruler or tape measure. Ask which object has the longest edge. Then lay 12 coins or cards in different rectangles and count the edge of each.","keyIdea":"The perimeter is the total distance all the way round the edge of a flat shape. Add the lengths of every side and keep the unit, such as cm or m. A rectangle has two pairs of equal sides, so its perimeter is length + width + length + width. If you know the perimeter and every side but one, subtract the sides you know. Two rectangles can have the same perimeter but different areas, or the same area but different perimeters.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is it about the perimeter or the area? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Perimeter (the edge)","Area (the inside)"],"items":[["A fence round a garden",0],["Grass seed to cover a lawn",1],["Ribbon round a card",0],["Carpet for a floor",1],["A frame round a picture",0],["Paint for a wall",1],["Tape round a box lid",0],["Tiles on a bathroom floor",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagperim","caption":"Flagship lab: walk round a rectangle, find missing sides, stretch a 24 cm rectangle and compare shapes made of 12 squares.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_0XxyaQLRhn":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which hand tells the hour?","choices":["The short hand","The long hand","Both hands"],"answer":0,"hint":"One hand is short and one is long. Which one points to the hour?","explain":"The short hand is the hour hand. The long hand shows the minutes."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"The long hand is on 6. The short hand is between 8 and 9. What time is it?","choices":["Half past 8","Half past 9","6 o'clock"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which number has the short hand just gone past?","explain":"The short hand has just passed 8 and the long hand is on 6, so it is half past 8."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Where is the long hand at 7 o'clock?","choices":["On the 12","On the 7","On the 6"],"answer":0,"hint":"O'clock times always have the long hand in the same place.","explain":"At any o'clock time the long hand points to 12. The short hand points to 7."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Where is the short hand at half past 2?","choices":["Halfway between 2 and 3","Right on the 2","On the 6"],"answer":0,"hint":"The short hand keeps moving while the long hand turns.","explain":"At half past 2 the short hand has moved halfway from 2 towards 3."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"It is {a} o'clock. The long hand goes all the way round {n} times. What o'clock is it now?","answer":"a+n","hint":"Each full turn of the long hand is 1 hour. Count on {n} hours from {a}.","explain":"{n} full turns is {n} hours, so {a} o'clock becomes {=a+n} o'clock.","params":{"a":[1,6],"n":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"How long does the long hand take to go all the way round?","choices":["1 hour","1 minute","12 hours"],"answer":0,"hint":"One full turn of the long hand moves the short hand on one number.","explain":"One full turn of the long hand takes 1 hour, which is 60 minutes."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"The long hand is on 12 and the short hand is on {h}. It is ___ o'clock.","answer":"h","hint":"With the long hand on 12, read the number the short hand points to.","explain":"The short hand points to {h}, so it is {h} o'clock.","params":{"h":[1,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Sam says the clock shows 12 o'clock. The long hand is on 12 and the short hand is on 3. What did Sam do?","choices":["Read the hands the wrong way round","Read it right","Forgot the half past"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which hand should Sam look at for the hour?","explain":"Sam read the long hand as the hour. The short hand is on 3, so it is 3 o'clock."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"The long hand is on 12. The short hand is on 4. What time is it?","choices":["4 o'clock","12 o'clock","Half past 4"],"answer":0,"hint":"The short hand tells the hour. The long hand on 12 means o'clock.","explain":"The short hand points to 4 and the long hand is on 12, so it is 4 o'clock."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"It is {h} o'clock. One hour passes. What o'clock is it now?","answer":"h+1","hint":"In one hour the short hand moves on to the next number.","explain":"One hour after {h} o'clock it is {=h+1} o'clock. The long hand went all the way round once.","params":{"h":[1,10]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"It is half past {h}. The short hand is between {h} and which number?","answer":"h+1","hint":"At half past, the short hand is halfway to the next number.","explain":"At half past {h} the short hand is halfway between {h} and {=h+1}.","params":{"h":[1,11]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"The long hand is on 6. What do we say?","choices":["Half past","O'clock","6 o'clock"],"answer":0,"hint":"The long hand has gone half way round the clock.","explain":"The long hand on 6 means half past. Then read the hour from the short hand."}],"explain":{"kid":["A clock has two hands. The short hand tells the hour. The long hand tells the minutes.","When the long hand points to 12, we say o'clock. Look where the short hand points. On the 3? It is 3 o'clock.","When the long hand goes all the way round, one hour has passed. The short hand moves on to the next number.","When the long hand points to 6, we say half past. The short hand is halfway to the next number. Just past 4? It is half past 4."],"adult":["In England this is Year 1 Measurement in the National Curriculum: tell the time to the hour and half past the hour, and draw the hands on a clock face to show these times. Children also learn the language before and after, and sequence events in time. In the US, Common Core 1.MD.B.3 asks first graders to tell and write time in hours and half-hours using analogue and digital clocks.","Why the hour hand moves: both hands turn all the time. The minute hand goes once round the face in 60 minutes, and in that time the hour hand travels from one number to the next. So at half past, the hour hand is halfway between two numbers, and the hour you say is the number it has just passed.","Common mistakes to watch for: children often read the hands the wrong way round, so 3 o'clock becomes 12 o'clock; they draw the hour hand right on the number at half past; at half past 4 they say half past 5 because the short hand is close to the 5; and they read the long hand on 6 as 6 o'clock. Always ask: which hand is the short one, and which number has it just passed?","In the lab your child turns the long hand once round and watches the short hand move on one number, reads three o'clock times, drags the short hand to make 7 o'clock and 2 o'clock, turns the long hand to the 6 to make half past 4, and reads three half-past clocks. Every step works by dragging on the clock or with big buttons."]},"familyTip":"Pick two times in your day, like 8 o'clock for school and half past 6 for dinner. At those times, look at a real clock together and ask: where is the long hand? Where is the short hand?","keyIdea":"An analogue clock has two hands. The short hand is the hour hand: it points to the hour. The long hand is the minute hand: when it points to 12 the time is o'clock, and when it points to 6 it is half past. While the long hand goes all the way round once, one hour passes and the short hand moves on one number, so at half past the short hand sits halfway between two numbers. Half past 4 means the short hand has just passed the 4, not reached the 5.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"O'clock or half past? Drag each one, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["O'clock","Half past"],"items":[["Long hand on 12",0],["Long hand on 6",1],["Short hand right on a number",0],["Short hand between two numbers",1],["3 o'clock",0],["Half past 3",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagclock","caption":"Flagship lab: turn the long hand to move the hour, read o'clock and half-past clocks, and drag the hands to set a time.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_-vvVxpOHG2":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Today is Monday. What day was yesterday?","choices":["Sunday","Tuesday","Monday"],"answer":0,"hint":"Yesterday is the day before today. What day comes before Monday?","explain":"Yesterday was the day before Monday: Sunday. Tuesday will be tomorrow."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which comes first?","choices":["The seed","The flower","They come together"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do you put in the soil to make a plant grow?","explain":"The seed comes first. Next a shoot grows, and last the flower opens."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"When do we usually have a bath and go to bed?","choices":["In the evening","In the morning","After breakfast"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it at the start of the day or the end?","explain":"Bath and bed come in the evening, at the end of the day."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"\"Eat your lunch after you wash your hands.\" What do you do first?","choices":["Wash my hands","Eat my lunch","Both at once"],"answer":0,"hint":"After tells you which job is later. Which job is after washing?","explain":"You hear 'eat' first, but 'after you wash' means you wash first, then eat."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"You made a snowman on day {d}. It melted {w} days later. On which day did it melt?","answer":"d+w","hint":"Start at day {d} and count on {w} more days.","explain":"Day {d} and {w} more days is day {=d+w}. It melted after you built it.","params":{"d":[1,3],"w":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Your party is the day after today. When is it?","choices":["Tomorrow","Yesterday","Today"],"answer":0,"hint":"Has the party happened yet, or is it still to come?","explain":"The day after today is tomorrow. It has not happened yet."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Today is day {d} of the holiday. What day of the holiday was yesterday?","answer":"d-1","hint":"Yesterday is one day before today. Count back one.","explain":"Yesterday was the day before today, so it was day {=d-1}.","params":{"d":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which is in the right order?","choices":["Wake up, eat breakfast, go to bed","Go to bed, wake up, eat breakfast","Eat breakfast, go to bed, wake up"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do you do at the very start of the day?","explain":"First you wake up, next you eat breakfast, and last, in the evening, you go to bed."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"What comes straight after morning?","choices":["Afternoon","Evening","Night"],"answer":0,"hint":"Lunch is in the middle of the day. What part comes after lunch?","explain":"Morning, then afternoon, then evening. Afternoon comes straight after morning."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"There are {n} pictures in a story. You have put {k} of them in order. How many are left to put in order?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Start at {n} and take away the {k} you have done.","explain":"{n} pictures take away {k} done leaves {=n-k} to put in order.","params":{"n":[4,6],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Today is day {d} of the holiday. What day of the holiday will tomorrow be?","answer":"d+1","hint":"Tomorrow is one day after today. Count on one.","explain":"Tomorrow is the day after today, so it is day {=d+1}.","params":{"d":[1,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"\"Before you go out, put your coat on.\" What do you do first?","choices":["Put my coat on","Go out","Take my coat off"],"answer":0,"hint":"Before tells you which job is earlier. Which job is before going out?","explain":"You hear 'go out' first, but 'before' means the coat goes on first, then you go out."}],"explain":{"kid":["Things happen in order. First you plant a seed. Next a shoot grows. Last you get a flower.","Before means it comes first. After means it comes next. Socks go on before shoes. Shoes go on after socks.","A day has parts. In the morning we eat breakfast. In the afternoon we play. In the evening we have a bath and go to bed.","Today is now. Yesterday is the day that has gone. Tomorrow is the day that has not come yet."],"adult":["In England this is Year 1 measurement in the National Curriculum: pupils should be taught to sequence events in chronological order using language such as before and after, next, first, today, yesterday, tomorrow, morning, afternoon and evening. It grows out of the EYFS reception goal of describing a sequence of events using words like first and then, and it leads on to days of the week, months and telling the time.","The key point is that time words describe the order things really happen in, not the order you say them. 'Eat your lunch after you wash your hands' mentions eating first, but you wash first. Yesterday and tomorrow are both 'not today', but one is behind you and one is ahead.","Common mistakes to watch for: young children often follow the order they hear, so 'before' and 'after' sentences get done back to front; they jump from morning straight to evening and forget the afternoon; and they swap yesterday and tomorrow, or use 'yesterday' for any time in the past. Asking 'has it happened yet?' sorts yesterday from tomorrow better than explaining.","In the lab your child drags picture cards into slots: a seed growing (first, next, last), two jobs given with before and after, the parts of a day as the sun moves across the sky, a three-day strip with today in the middle, and a four-picture snowman story. Every card can also be placed with a big button. Read each sentence aloud together."]},"familyTip":"At bedtime, ask your child to tell you three things from today in order, using first, next and last. Then ask: what will happen tomorrow? Try a 'before and after' game while dressing: 'Before your coat, what goes on?'","keyIdea":"Things happen in an order. Time words tell us that order. First, next and last say which thing comes when. Before means earlier and after means later. A day goes morning, then afternoon, then evening. Yesterday was the day before today, and tomorrow is the day after today.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Morning or evening? Drag each one, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Morning","Evening"],"items":[["Eat breakfast",0],["Wake up",0],["Get dressed",0],["Have a bath",1],["Read a bedtime story",1],["Put on pyjamas",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagorder","caption":"Flagship lab: drag picture cards into order with first, next, last, before and after, morning to evening, and yesterday, today and tomorrow.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_6xNmQLzuqm":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A shape is covered by {a} rows of unit squares with {b} squares in each row. What is its area in square units?","answer":"a*b","hint":"Each row has {b} squares. Add one row at a time.","explain":"{a} rows of {b} make {=a*b} unit squares, so the area is {=a*b} square units.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[3,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"A shape has an area of {a} square units. You move one of its squares to a new spot that still touches the shape. What is the area now?","answer":"a","hint":"Did you add a square or take one away?","explain":"The same {a} unit squares are still there, just in a different place, so the area is still {a} square units. Only the perimeter can change.","params":{"a":[5,12]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"What does 'one square unit' mean?","choices":["The area of a square with sides 1 unit long","A line 1 unit long","Any tile, whatever its size"],"answer":0,"hint":"A unit square has sides of 1 unit.","explain":"One square unit is the area of a unit square, a square with each side 1 unit long. A line 1 unit long is a length, not an area."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Ava covers a rectangle with 8 tiles, but some tiles are big and some are small. Is its area 8 square units?","choices":["No, the tiles must all be the same unit square","Yes, 8 tiles is 8 square units","Yes, if the big tiles are at the edge"],"answer":0,"hint":"Would a big tile and a small tile count as the same amount of space?","explain":"No. Mixed sizes do not give a fair count, because a big tile covers more space than a small one. Use equal unit squares."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Shape A is 6 squares long and 1 square wide. Shape B is 3 squares long and 2 squares wide. Which has the bigger area?","choices":["They have the same area, 6 square units","Shape A, because it is longer","Shape B, because it is wider"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the unit squares in each shape.","explain":"A has 6 squares and B has 2 rows of 3, which is also 6. Both are 6 square units. Looking longer does not mean more area."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"A shape has 5 whole squares and 4 half squares. Leo says the area is 9 square units. What should it be?","choices":["7 square units","9 square units","5 square units"],"answer":0,"hint":"Two half squares make one whole square.","explain":"The 4 halves make 2 whole squares, so the area is 5 + 2 = 7 square units. Leo counted each half as a whole."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A rectangle is 4 squares long and 2 squares wide. Which is its area?","choices":["8 square units","12 units","6 square units"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the squares inside, not the length of the edge.","explain":"2 rows of 4 make 8 unit squares, so the area is 8 square units. 12 units is the perimeter, the distance round the edge, and 6 adds the two sides."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"A rectangle is {a} squares long and {b} squares wide. How many square units is its area?","answer":"a*b","hint":"It has {b} rows, and each row has {a} squares. Count them all, or add the rows.","explain":"{b} rows of {a} unit squares make {=a*b} squares, so the area is {=a*b} square units.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[2,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A shape is made of {a} whole squares and {=2*b} half squares. What is its area in square units?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Two half squares make one whole square. How many wholes do the {=2*b} halves make?","explain":"{=2*b} halves make {b} whole squares. {a} + {b} = {=a+b}, so the area is {=a+b} square units.","params":{"a":[3,9],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Kai covers a shape with 9 circles and says its area is 9 square units. What is wrong?","choices":["Circles leave gaps, so they do not measure area fairly","Nothing, 9 circles is 9 square units","He should have counted the edge instead"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can circles fit together with no gaps between them?","explain":"Circles leave gaps, so some of the shape is not counted. Area must be measured with unit squares that fit with no gaps and no overlaps."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"You want to buy a fence to go round a garden. What do you need to measure?","choices":["The perimeter","The area","The number of plants"],"answer":0,"hint":"Does the fence cover the garden, or go round its edge?","explain":"A fence goes round the edge, so you need the perimeter. Area would tell you how much grass or soil is inside."}],"explain":{"kid":["Area is how much flat space a shape covers. Think of the floor of your room. A rug that covers more of the floor has a bigger area.","To measure area we use a unit square. It is a square with sides 1 unit long, like 1 cm. Its area is 1 square unit. Cover the shape with unit squares and count them. A rectangle 4 squares long and 3 squares wide holds 3 rows of 4, so its area is 12 square units.","The count is only fair if the squares are all the same size, with no gaps and no overlaps. Circles leave gaps. Tiles on top of each other get counted twice. If you move a square to a new spot, the area stays the same, but the edge can get longer.","Area is not perimeter. Perimeter is the length all the way round the edge, like a fence. Area is the space inside, like a carpet. Some shapes have half squares. Two halves make one whole square unit, so count the wholes and then pair up the halves."],"adult":["The US Common Core starts area in Grade 3. It says 'a square with side length 1 unit, called \"a unit square,\" is said to have \"one square unit\" of area'. A shape that can be covered 'without gaps or overlaps by n unit squares' has an area of n square units (3.MD.5). Children measure it 'by counting unit squares' (3.MD.6). In England, Year 4 pupils 'find the area of rectilinear shapes by counting squares'. Year 5 then works out the area of rectangles in cm² and m².","Area measures flat space, so the unit must be an area too. Counting only works when the units fit the shape exactly. They must be equal squares, with no gaps and no overlaps. Seeing a rectangle as rows of equal squares (3 rows of 4) leads to length × width later. If you move the same squares into a new shape, the area stays the same, even when the perimeter changes.","Common mistakes: mixing up area and perimeter (counting the squares round the edge, or adding the sides); counting mixed sizes, circles or overlapping tiles as if they were unit squares; thinking a longer shape must have more area; counting each half square as a whole, or leaving halves out; and giving no unit, or cm instead of square cm.","In the lab learners drag across a grid to cover shapes. They judge whether a tiling gives a fair count. They move one square and see the area stay put while the perimeter changes. They decide whether a job needs area or perimeter, and they pair up half squares."]},"familyTip":"Use sticky notes or square crackers as unit squares. Cover a book cover or a tray and count them, then ask: would it be fair to use some big and some small ones? Move one cracker to stick out at the side and ask whether there is more cracker now.","keyIdea":"Area is the amount of flat surface a shape covers. We measure it in unit squares: a square with sides of 1 unit has an area of 1 square unit. Cover the shape with unit squares, with no gaps and no overlaps, and count them. Area is different from perimeter, which is the distance round the edge.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does the job need area or perimeter? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Area","Perimeter"],"items":[["Carpet for a bedroom floor",0],["A fence round a field",1],["Paint for a wall",0],["A border round a noticeboard",1],["Grass seed for a lawn",0],["A frame round a photo",1],["Wrapping paper for the top of a box",0],["Lace round the edge of a tablecloth",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcover","caption":"Flagship lab: cover shapes with unit squares, judge fair tilings, move a square, tell area from perimeter, pair half squares.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_bj1YCgNWUx":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"How many days are in one week?","choices":["7","5","12"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say the days from Monday to Sunday and count them on your fingers.","explain":"Monday to Sunday is 7 days. School days may be 5, but Saturday and Sunday count too. 12 is the months in a year."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"How many months are in a year?","choices":["12","7","52"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say the months from January to December and count them.","explain":"January to December is 12 months. 7 is the days in a week, and 52 is about the weeks in a year."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Today is Saturday. What day was yesterday?","choices":["Friday","Sunday","Thursday"],"answer":0,"hint":"Yesterday is the day just before today.","explain":"The day before Saturday is Friday. Sunday is tomorrow, and Thursday was two days ago."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which month comes after June?","choices":["July","May","August"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say the months in order: April, May, June, then what?","explain":"July comes straight after June. May comes before June, and August is after July."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Today is Thursday. What day will it be in 3 days?","choices":["Sunday","Saturday","Monday"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do not count today. Hop: Friday is 1, then keep going.","explain":"Friday is 1, Saturday is 2, Sunday is 3. Saturday comes from counting Thursday as 1, and Monday is one hop too many."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which is longer: a week or a month?","choices":["a month","a week","they are the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about how many weeks fit in one month.","explain":"A month has 28 to 31 days, which is a bit more than 4 weeks. A week is only 7 days."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A school trip is in {a} weeks. Each week has 7 days. How many days away is the trip?","answer":"a*7","hint":"Each week is 7 days. Count on 7 for each week.","explain":"{a} weeks of 7 days is {=a*7} days, so the trip is {=a*7} days away.","params":{"a":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"The shop is open {a} days a week. How many days a week is it closed?","answer":"7-a","hint":"A week has 7 days. Take away the days it is open.","explain":"7 − {a} = {=7-a}, so the shop is closed {=7-a} days a week.","params":{"a":[3,6]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Today is Wednesday. What day will it be tomorrow?","choices":["Thursday","Tuesday","Friday"],"answer":0,"hint":"Tomorrow is the day after today. Say the days in order from Wednesday.","explain":"The day after Wednesday is Thursday. Tuesday was yesterday, and Friday is two days away."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A week has 7 days. You have already had {a} days of this week. How many days of the week are left?","answer":"7-a","hint":"Start from 7 days and take away the days that have gone.","explain":"7 − {a} = {=7-a}, so {=7-a} days of the week are left. Then a new week starts.","params":{"a":[1,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A year has 12 months. {a} months of this year have gone. How many months are left?","answer":"12-a","hint":"Start from 12 months and take away the months that have gone.","explain":"12 − {a} = {=12-a}, so {=12-a} months are left before a new year starts in January.","params":{"a":[2,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which comes after Sunday?","choices":["Monday","Saturday","No day: the week is over"],"answer":0,"hint":"The days go round like a wheel. Where does the week start again?","explain":"After Sunday the week starts again, so Monday comes next. Saturday comes before Sunday."}],"explain":{"kid":["A week has 7 days. They go Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, Saturday, Sunday. Then the week starts again with Monday.","Today is the day we are in now. Yesterday was the day before. Tomorrow is the day after. If today is Tuesday, yesterday was Monday and tomorrow is Wednesday.","To find the day in 2 days, start on today and hop. The first hop is tomorrow. Do not count today as a hop.","A month is much longer than a week. It is about 4 weeks. There are 12 months in a year. They go from January to December, then a new year starts. Your birthday comes once a year."],"adult":["England's Year 1 maths programme asks children to 'recognise and use language relating to dates, including days of the week, weeks, months and years', alongside sequencing events in time. Year 2 adds that there are 60 minutes in an hour and 24 hours in a day. The aim at this age is the names in order and how the units nest, not reading a full calendar.","The facts to hold: 7 days in a week, 12 months in a year, 28 to 31 days in a month, 365 days in a year (366 in a leap year), which is 52 weeks and 1 or 2 days. A month is therefore a little over 4 weeks. UK and ISO calendars start the week on Monday, and many US calendars start on Sunday, but the cycle is the same.","Common mistakes: thinking the week ends after Sunday instead of going round, saying 5 days in a week because of school days, mixing up 7 days and 12 months, swapping yesterday and tomorrow, and counting today as the first day when working out 'in 3 days'. Children also often think a month and a week are about the same length.","In the lab your child builds the week wheel and the year wheel by tapping the next name, turns a Today arrow past Sunday, hops on to find a day in a few days, and fills a 30-day April with whole weeks before filling a year with months."]},"familyTip":"Each morning, ask: what day is it today, what was yesterday, and what will tomorrow be? Hang a simple calendar low down and let your child cross off each day. Sing the days of the week or the months while you walk, and count down the days to something exciting by hopping on from tomorrow.","keyIdea":"Time comes in sizes. A day is one morning, afternoon, evening and night. Seven days make a week: Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday, Thursday, Friday, Saturday and Sunday. About four weeks make a month, and twelve months, from January to December, make a year. The days and the months go round in the same order, so after Sunday comes Monday and after December comes January.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is it a day of the week or a month? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Day of the week","Month"],"items":[["Monday",0],["Friday",0],["Sunday",0],["Wednesday",0],["March",1],["July",1],["October",1],["December",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcal","caption":"Flagship lab: build the week wheel, turn past Sunday, hop on to a future day, build the year wheel and fill a month with weeks.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_TcG90kS8nu":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which takes the shortest time?","choices":["a sneeze","a night of sleep","a day at school"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one is over almost as soon as it starts?","explain":"A sneeze is over in a moment. A night of sleep and a school day both go on for hours."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A song takes 6 claps. A dance takes 6 claps. Which is true?","choices":["they take the same time","the song takes longer","the dance takes longer"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare the number of claps for each one.","explain":"6 claps and 6 claps is the same number, so the song and the dance take the same time."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"The bath starts first. The snack starts later and ends last. How can you tell which takes longer?","choices":["count the claps for each one","the one that ends last takes longer","the one that starts first takes longer"],"answer":0,"hint":"The end alone does not tell you. What do you need to know about the start too?","explain":"The snack may end last only because it started late. Count the claps from each start, or line up the starts, then compare."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Two children race. Who is slower?","choices":["the one who takes more claps","the one who takes fewer claps","the taller child"],"answer":0,"hint":"More claps means more time.","explain":"Taking more claps means taking more time, so that child is slower. Being tall does not tell you the time."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"The sand timer runs out before the song ends. Which takes longer?","choices":["the song","the sand timer","they take the same time"],"answer":0,"hint":"They started together. Which one is still going?","explain":"The song is still playing when the sand has run out, so the song takes longer."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Putting on shoes takes {a} claps. Putting on a coat takes {b} claps. How many claps for both, one after the other?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Add the claps for the shoes and the claps for the coat.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b}, so both jobs together take {=a+b} claps.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[2,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A hop race takes {a} claps. A walk takes {b} claps more. How many claps does the walk take?","answer":"a+b","hint":"Start at the hop race claps and count on.","explain":"{a} + {b} = {=a+b}. The walk takes more claps, so it takes a longer time.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Put these in order from the shortest time to the longest: lunch, sneeze, putting on shoes.","choices":["sneeze, shoes, lunch","lunch, shoes, sneeze","shoes, sneeze, lunch"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the quickest one first, then the next, then the longest.","explain":"A sneeze is the shortest, putting on shoes takes a little while, and lunch takes the longest."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which takes longer: eating your lunch or blinking your eyes?","choices":["eating your lunch","blinking your eyes","they take the same time"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about starting both at the same time. Which one is still going?","explain":"A blink is over in a moment. Eating lunch keeps going for a long time, so it takes longer."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A song takes {a} claps. Tidy up takes {b} claps. How many more claps does the song take?","answer":"a-b","hint":"Start from the bigger number of claps and count back the smaller number.","explain":"{a} − {b} = {=a-b}, so the song takes {=a-b} more claps. More claps means a longer time.","params":{"a":[8,10],"b":[2,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Mia runs in {a} claps. Sam takes 2 more claps than Mia. How many claps does Sam take?","answer":"a+2","hint":"Start at Mia's claps and count on 2 more.","explain":"{a} + 2 = {=a+2}. Sam takes more claps, so Sam is slower and Mia is quicker.","params":{"a":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Ben tidies up in 4 claps. Ava tidies up in 7 claps. Who is quicker?","choices":["Ben","Ava","they are the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fewer claps means less time.","explain":"Ben takes 4 claps, fewer than 7, so Ben is quicker. Ava takes more time, so she is slower."}],"explain":{"kid":["Some things take a long time, like a night of sleep. Some take a short time, like a sneeze.","Start two things at the same time. Which one is still going when the other stops? That one takes longer.","We can count claps to measure time. Clap at the same speed. 6 claps and 6 claps is the same time. Fewer claps is quicker. More claps is slower.","Be careful. The one that ends last may have started late. Line up the starts, then look."],"adult":["England's Year 1 maths programme asks children to 'compare, describe and solve practical problems for time [for example, quicker, slower, earlier, later]' and to 'measure and begin to record time (hours, minutes, seconds)'. In the Early Years this starts as talk: which takes longer, which is shorter, the same time. Duration means how long an event lasts, from its start to its end.","Young children judge time the way they judge length: they compare by eye and by the end point. Just as two sticks must start at the same line to compare lengths, two events must start together, or be measured in the same units, before one can be called longer. Non-standard units such as claps, a sand timer or counting to ten come before seconds and minutes, and they only work if the beat stays steady.","Common mistakes: children often decide that the event which finishes last took the longest, even when it started later. In a race, many think more claps means quicker, but more claps means more time, so slower. Some mix up a long time with something big or far away. Others say two events are different when they take the same number of claps.","In the lab your child presses Play, so both bars grow together, and taps the one still going. Next they stretch a bar until two jobs take the same time. Then they slide a late snack back to the start line. Last, they pick the quicker runner by fewer claps and order three jobs from shortest to longest time."]},"familyTip":"Race small jobs against each other: who can put their shoes on before the song ends? Clap or count together while two things happen, then ask which took longer. Use the words quicker, slower, longer and shorter time, and a sand timer or a kitchen timer to check.","keyIdea":"A duration is how long something takes. To compare two durations, start them at the same time: the one still going when the other stops takes longer, and the other takes a shorter time. We can measure time by counting steady claps. The same number of claps means the same time. In a race, the one who takes fewer claps is quicker and the one who takes more is slower. Ending last does not mean taking longest if it started late.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Does it take a short time or a long time? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Short time","Long time"],"items":[["Blink",0],["Sneeze",0],["Clap once",0],["Blow out a candle",0],["Sleep at night",1],["A day at school",1],["Grow a tree",1],["A long car trip",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagdur","caption":"Flagship lab: start two jobs together, make two jobs take the same time, line up a late start, pick the quicker runner and order three jobs by time.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_HNOPGJYiRK":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"How many minutes from one number to the next on a clock?","choices":["5","1","10"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the little marks between the 12 and the 1.","explain":"There are 5 small marks from one number to the next, so each number is 5 minutes more."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Long hand on the 6, short hand between the 7 and the 8. What time is it?","choices":["half past 7","half past 8","6 o'clock"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use the hour the short hand has just passed.","explain":"The long hand on 6 is half past. The short hand has passed 7 but not reached 8, so it is half past 7."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Twenty to 3 in numbers is…","choices":["2:40","3:20","3:40"],"answer":0,"hint":"Twenty to 3 is 20 minutes before 3 o'clock, so it is still in the 2 o'clock hour.","explain":"20 minutes before 3:00 is 2:40, because 40 + 20 = 60."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"At quarter past, the long hand points to the…","choices":["3","15","9"],"answer":0,"hint":"There is no 15 on the clock face. Count in 5s to 15.","explain":"5, 10, 15 takes the long hand to the 3."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Ella says the long hand on the 4 means 4 minutes past. What is her mistake?","choices":["each number is 5 minutes, so it is 20 past","nothing, it is 4 minutes past","it is 40 minutes past"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count in 5s from the 12 to the 4.","explain":"5, 10, 15, 20. The 4 stands for 20 minutes, so it is 20 past."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"At quarter to 6, where is the short hand?","choices":["nearly on the 6","right on the 6","on the 9"],"answer":0,"hint":"Quarter to 6 is not quite 6 o'clock yet.","explain":"The short hand moves slowly. At quarter to 6 it is three quarters of the way from 5 to 6."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"The long hand points to the {n}. How many minutes to the next hour?","answer":"60-n*5","hint":"Count in 5s from the long hand on round to the 12.","explain":"{n} is {=n*5} minutes past, and 60 − {=n*5} = {=60-n*5} minutes to the next hour.","params":{"n":[7,11]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"School starts at 9:00. It is now {m} minutes past 8. How many minutes until school starts?","answer":"60-m","hint":"One hour is 60 minutes. Count on from the minutes now to 60.","explain":"60 − {m} = {=60-m} minutes until 9 o'clock.","params":{"m":[35,55]},"where":"m%5==0"}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"The long hand points to the 3. How many minutes past the hour?","choices":["15","3","30"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count in 5s from the 12: 5, 10, …","explain":"5, 10, 15. The 3 means 3 jumps of 5, which is 15 minutes: quarter past."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"The long hand is on the 9 and the short hand is nearly on the 4. What time is it?","choices":["quarter to 4","quarter past 9","quarter to 3"],"answer":0,"hint":"The long hand on the 9 means 15 minutes to go. Which hour is coming next?","explain":"Long hand on 9 is quarter to. The short hand is nearly on 4, so it is quarter to 4, which is 3:45."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"How do you write quarter past 2 in numbers?","choices":["2:15","2:25","3:15"],"answer":0,"hint":"Quarter past means 15 minutes after the hour.","explain":"Quarter past 2 is 15 minutes after 2 o'clock, so it is 2:15."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"The long hand points to the {n}. How many minutes past the hour is that?","answer":"n*5","hint":"Each number is 5 minutes. Count in 5s from the 12 until you reach the number.","explain":"{n} jumps of 5 minutes is {=n*5} minutes past the hour.","params":{"n":[1,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"It is {m} minutes past 3. How many minutes until 4 o'clock?","answer":"60-m","hint":"An hour has 60 minutes. Take away the minutes that have gone.","explain":"60 − {m} = {=60-m}, so it is {=60-m} minutes to 4.","params":{"m":[5,55]},"where":"m%5==0"}],"explain":{"kid":["A clock has a long hand and a short hand. The long hand tells the minutes. The short hand tells the hour.","Count the long hand in 5s. Start at the 12 and say 5, 10, 15, 20 as you go round. If it points to the 4, it is 20 minutes past.","Long hand on the 3 is quarter past. Long hand on the 9 is quarter to. Quarter to 4 means 15 more minutes until 4 o'clock.","The short hand moves too, just slowly. At half past 7 it is in the middle, between the 7 and the 8."],"adult":["Year 2 maths in England asks children to tell and write the time to five minutes, including quarter past and quarter to the hour, and to draw the hands on a clock face to show these times. Year 1 covered o'clock and half past. In the US, first grade covers hours and half hours and second grade (2.MD.7) covers the nearest five minutes.","Words to use: minute hand (long hand), hour hand (short hand), minutes past, minutes to, quarter past, half past, quarter to, o'clock, and the digital form such as 3:45. Counting in 5s is the bridge: each numeral on the face stands for 5 minutes, and 12 jumps of 5 make 60 minutes, one hour.","Common mistakes: reading the number the long hand points to as the minutes (the 4 read as 4 minutes, not 20); swapping the hands; giving the wrong hour for 'to' times (calling 3:45 'quarter to 3' instead of 'quarter to 4'); and expecting the hour hand to sit exactly on a number all hour. Show that the short hand creeps forward as the long hand goes round.","In the lab your child counts round the clock in 5s, drags the minute hand to quarter past and quarter to, sees which side of the clock says 'past' and which says 'to', and watches the hour hand move with it."]},"familyTip":"Use a real clock or a paper plate clock with a split pin. Ask 'Where will the long hand be at quarter past?' before you turn it. At home, say times aloud: 'It is twenty past 5, tea in ten minutes.' Point to where the long hand will be when tea is ready.","keyIdea":"The long hand is the minute hand. From one number to the next is 5 minutes, so count the long hand in 5s from the 12: the 1 is 5 minutes past, the 3 is 15 (quarter past), the 6 is 30 (half past) and the 9 is 45 (quarter to the next hour). On the right of the clock we say minutes past the hour; on the left we say minutes to the next hour. The short hand moves slowly between the numbers, so at quarter to 4 it is nearly on the 4.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Past or to? Drag each time, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Minutes past","Minutes to"],"items":[["Long hand on 3",0],["Long hand on 9",1],["Long hand on 2",0],["Long hand on 8",1],["Quarter past 6",0],["Twenty to 4",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagmins","caption":"Flagship lab: count the minute hand in 5s, drag it to quarter past and quarter to, and watch the hour hand creep to the next hour.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_KaF0SQvaiu":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Length is…","choices":["how long something is along a line","how loud it is","how heavy it is always"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think end to end in space.","explain":"Length measures distance along a line — not sound or mass."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A shifted start makes…","choices":["an unfair compare","a better measure","the object heavier"],"answer":0,"hint":"Starts must line up before any fair compare.","explain":"If starts do not line up, the compare is unfair. It does not change mass."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Height is about…","choices":["how tall something stands","only how wide it is","how colourful it is"],"answer":0,"hint":"Up from the ground or base.","explain":"Height is standing tall. Width and colour are different."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Why use the same unit?","choices":["so the count means the same each time","so it looks pretty","so you can skip lining up"],"answer":0,"hint":"Units must be consistent.","explain":"Same-size units make counts comparable. Looks and skipping line-up are not the goal."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Tower A is {n} cubes tall. Tower B is {k} cubes tall. How many cubes taller is A?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Subtract B’s height from A’s.","explain":"{n} − {k} = {=n-k} cubes taller.","params":{"n":[6,9],"k":[2,5]},"where":"n>k"},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"You need {n} cubes to measure a book. How many cubes do you need?","answer":"n","hint":"Use one cube per unit along the length.","explain":"You need {n} cubes for a fair end-to-end measure.","params":{"n":[4,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Longer means…","choices":["covers more along the line","stands higher up always","has more colour"],"answer":0,"hint":"Longer means more distance along a line.","explain":"Longer covers more end to end. Taller is height; colour is unrelated."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Mixing huge and tiny units in one count is…","choices":["unfair","the best method","required for height only"],"answer":0,"hint":"Every unit in one measure must be the same size.","explain":"Mixed unit sizes make the number meaningless. Keep units the same."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Before comparing two lengths, you should…","choices":["line up the starting ends","only look at the far ends","flip one object over"],"answer":0,"hint":"Fair compares share the same start.","explain":"Line up the starts first. Comparing only far ends (or flipping) makes the compare unfair."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Taller means…","choices":["stands higher up","is always longer flat on the table","weighs more"],"answer":0,"hint":"Height is about how high something stands.","explain":"Taller is height. Longer is along a line. Weight is a different attribute."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A ribbon is {n} cubes long. Another is {k} cubes long. How much longer is the first?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Subtract the shorter cube count from the longer.","explain":"{n} − {k} = {=n-k} cubes longer.","params":{"n":[5,8],"k":[2,4]},"where":"n>k"},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You place {n} cubes end to end. How many units long is the object?","answer":"n","hint":"Each cube is one unit when they touch with no gaps.","explain":"The object measures {n} cube units.","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A fair cube measure needs…","choices":["cubes touching end to end","big random gaps","different sized units mixed"],"answer":0,"hint":"Same unit, no gaps or overlaps.","explain":"Equal cubes must touch end to end. Gaps or mixed sizes make the count unfair."}],"explain":{"kid":["Length means how long something is from end to end. Height means how tall it stands.","Before you compare, line up the starting ends. Looking only at the far ends is not fair.","You can measure with cubes or hand spans. Use the same unit each time, touching end to end with no gaps.","Longer is not the same word as taller. A long ribbon can lie flat. A tall tower stands up."],"adult":["Year 1 measure: compare, describe and solve practical problems for lengths and heights; measure with non-standard units. Words: length, height, longer, shorter, taller, unit, line up, gap, overlap.","Common mistakes: comparing only far ends; leaving gaps between units; mixing unit sizes; swapping taller with longer; thinking heavier means longer.","In the lab your child lines up starts, picks longer vs shorter and taller vs shorter, lays cubes along a bar, fixes an unfair start/unit choice, then faces challenge traps for those slips."]},"familyTip":"Put two pencils side by side. Ask: 'Did we line up the starts?' Then measure a book with cubes or paper clips: 'How many cubes long?'","keyIdea":"Length is how long something is along a line. Height is how tall it stands. To compare fairly, line up the starting ends. Measure with the same non-standard unit end to end — no gaps or overlaps.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Fair measure or unfair? Drag each clue, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Fair measure","Unfair measure"],"items":[["starts lined up, cubes end to end",0],["only comparing the far ends",1],["same-size cubes with no gaps",0],["big random gaps between units",1],["one start line for both objects",0],["mixing huge and tiny units in one count",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flaglenht","caption":"Flagship lab: line up starts, compare longer and taller, measure with cubes, and catch unfair-measure slips.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_4lp_b5Pzik":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Holds more means…","choices":["takes more cups when full","is always taller","is half empty now"],"answer":0,"hint":"Compare how many equal cups each container takes when full.","explain":"Greater capacity means more cups when full."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A capacity record needs…","choices":["a number and a unit","only a doodle","the word empty alone"],"answer":0,"hint":"Write both the number and the unit together, like 4 cups.","explain":"Write the number with its unit."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Litres are…","choices":["units for larger capacities","only used for length","the same as counting leaves"],"answer":0,"hint":"Big bottles and jugs often show litres on the label.","explain":"Litres measure capacity for larger amounts — not length."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"How full now is…","choices":["not the same as capacity","exactly the capacity always","why we skip measuring"],"answer":0,"hint":"A half-full snapshot is not how much the jug can hold.","explain":"A half-full jug still has the same capacity."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Jug A holds {n} cups. Jug B holds {k} cups. How many more cups does A hold?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Subtract jug B’s cup count from jug A’s cup count.","explain":"{n} − {k} = {=n-k} cups more.","params":{"n":[6,9],"k":[2,5]},"where":"n>k"},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"You need {n} cups to fill a pot. How many cups is that?","answer":"n","hint":"The capacity in cups is how many you need when it is full.","explain":"The pot’s capacity is {n} cups.","params":{"n":[3,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Why not trust height alone?","choices":["shape matters — measure with cups","taller always wins","cups never help"],"answer":0,"hint":"A tall thin jug can hold less — measure by counting equal cups.","explain":"Count equal cups; do not rank by height only."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Using unequal random cups is…","choices":["unfair","the best method","required for litres"],"answer":0,"hint":"Every pour must use the same cup size for a fair count.","explain":"Same cup size each pour. Mixed cups make the count unfair."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Capacity means…","choices":["how much it can hold when full","how full it is right now","only how tall it looks"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the full amount the container can hold, not a snapshot.","explain":"Capacity is how much a container can hold when full — not how full it looks now."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"To measure with cups…","choices":["count equal cups that fill it","use random different cups","never pour anything"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use the same cup size and pour until the jug is full.","explain":"Equal cups poured until full give a fair capacity measure."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A jug takes {n} cups. A bowl takes {k} cups. How many cups in all to fill both?","answer":"n+k","hint":"Add the jug capacity and the bowl capacity together.","explain":"{n} + {k} = {=n+k} cups to fill both.","params":{"n":[2,5],"k":[2,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A jug holds {n} cups. You have poured {k}. How many cups left to fill it?","answer":"n-k","hint":"Subtract the cups you have already poured from the full capacity.","explain":"Capacity {n}; poured {k}; {=n-k} cups left.","params":{"n":[5,8],"k":[1,4]},"where":"n>k"},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A tall thin jug…","choices":["might hold less than a short wide one","always holds the most","cannot be measured"],"answer":0,"hint":"Shape matters — count equal cups rather than trusting height alone.","explain":"Height alone misleads. Measure with equal cups."}],"explain":{"kid":["Capacity means how much a pot or jug can hold when it is full.","You can measure it by pouring the same cup again and again and counting the cups.","Write the answer with a number and a unit — like 4 cups. Big jugs may be about 1 litre.","A tall thin jug can hold less than a short wide one. How full it is right now is not its capacity."],"adult":["Year 1 Maths (measurement): measure and begin to record capacity/volume using non-standard units and litres. Distinct from qualitative full/empty language in 'Heavy and light, full and empty'. Words: capacity, volume, cup, litre, holds more, when full.","Common mistakes: equating capacity with how full something looks now; assuming taller always holds more; mixing unequal cups; recording a number with no unit.","In the lab your child fills by cup count, compares capacities, records cups and litres, rejects full-now snapshots, picks short-wide over tall-thin when cups say so, then faces challenge traps for those slips."]},"familyTip":"Fill a jug using the same cup. Count: '1 cup, 2 cups…' Ask: 'Is the tall thin bottle really holding more than the short wide bowl?'","keyIdea":"Capacity is how much a container can hold when full. Measure it by counting equal cups that fill it, then record a number with a unit (cups, and litres for larger jugs). Taller does not always mean more; how full right now is not capacity.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Capacity idea or not? Drag each clue, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["About capacity","Not capacity"],"items":[["count cups that fill the jug",0],["how full it looks this second",1],["write 4 cups as the measure",0],["taller always means more",1],["use litres for a big jug",0],["only guess from height",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcups","caption":"Flagship lab: fill by counting cups, compare who holds more, record units, separate full-now from capacity, and catch taller=more slips.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_zd0YkB3xNj":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"On a balance, the heavier side…","choices":["goes down","floats up","cannot be compared"],"answer":0,"hint":"Gravity pulls the heavier pan lower.","explain":"The heavier pan goes down."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A mass record needs…","choices":["a number and a unit","only a doodle","the word empty alone"],"answer":0,"hint":"Say how many and of what.","explain":"Write e.g. 4 cubes or 20 grams."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A big balloon compared with a small stone…","choices":["might be lighter — measure to check","is always heavier","cannot sit on a balance"],"answer":0,"hint":"Size is not the same as mass — check on a balance.","explain":"A big light object can lose to a small heavy one; measure to be sure."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Using random different-sized cubes…","choices":["makes the measure unfair","is the best method","is required by every scale"],"answer":0,"hint":"Every cube must be the same size so each unit means the same.","explain":"Unequal cubes break the unit; keep cubes identical."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A pointer on a cube scale points at {n}. What mass reading is that?","answer":"n","hint":"Read the number the pointer aims at.","explain":"Pointer at {n} means mass {n} cubes.","params":{"n":[2,10]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Object A balances {a} cubes. Object B balances {b} cubes. How many more cubes is B than A?","answer":"b-a","hint":"Subtract the smaller cube count from the larger.","explain":"B is {=b-a} cubes heavier than A.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[6,9]},"where":"b>a"},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Grams are…","choices":["standard units for mass","only used for length","the same as counting leaves"],"answer":0,"hint":"Kitchen scales often mark mass in grams.","explain":"Grams are a standard unit for how heavy something is."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Why use a balance with cubes?","choices":["to measure mass in equal units","to make the object taller","to fill a cup with water"],"answer":0,"hint":"Cubes are equal non-standard mass units.","explain":"Balancing equal cubes measures mass."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Mass means…","choices":["how heavy something is","how tall something is","how full a cup looks now"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about the pull of weight, not height.","explain":"Mass is how heavy an object is."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"To measure mass with cubes…","choices":["add equal cubes until it balances","only look at how big it is","guess without a balance"],"answer":0,"hint":"Equal units and a level balance give the measure.","explain":"Add same-size cubes until both sides level, then count them."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A toy balances {n} equal cubes. What mass do you record in cubes?","answer":"n","hint":"The cube count at balance is the mass in cube units.","explain":"Balanced with {n} cubes means the mass is {n} cubes.","params":{"n":[3,8]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A bag balances {n} cubes. You add {k} more identical cubes by mistake so it tips. How many cubes were the true mass?","answer":"n","hint":"The true mass is the count that first made both sides level.","explain":"True mass was {n} cubes; the extra {k} tipped it past balance.","params":{"n":[4,7],"k":[1,2]},"where":"n>k"},{"type":"order","id":"p5","prompt":"Put the mass-measuring steps in order.","items":["place the object on one pan","add equal cubes on the other pan","stop when both sides are level","write the number of cubes with the unit"],"hint":"Object first, then cubes, then level, then record.","explain":"Balance first, then record number + unit."}],"explain":{"kid":["Mass means how heavy something is.","Put the thing on one side of a balance. Add the same size cubes on the other side until both sides are level.","Count the cubes. Write the number and the word cubes. That is the mass in cube units.","A big balloon can be lighter than a small stone. Measure — do not trust size alone."],"adult":["Year 1 measure: mass/weight with non-standard units, then begin standard units (grams). Words: mass, weight, balance, heavier, lighter, cubes, grams, unit, scale.","Common mistakes: thinking bigger always means heavier; recording a number with no unit; using unequal cubes; reading the wrong mark on a scale; confusing how full something looks with how heavy it is.","In the lab your child tips a balance, adds cubes until level, records 4 cubes and grams, picks the heavier stone over a big balloon, reads a pointer at 6, then faces challenge traps for those slips."]},"familyTip":"Put a book on one side of a kitchen scale or homemade balance and add identical building cubes on the other until it balances. Ask: 'How many cubes is the book?' Then try a big empty box versus a small tin of beans.","keyIdea":"Mass is how heavy something is. Measure it by balancing equal cubes until both sides level, then record a number with a unit (for example 4 cubes). Simple scales can also show grams. Bigger is not always heavier — measure, do not guess from size alone.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Fair mass measure or not? Drag each clue, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Fair measure","Unfair / myth"],"items":[["add equal cubes until it balances",0],["only guess from how big it looks",1],["write 4 cubes as the mass",0],["bigger always means heavier",1],["read grams on a simple scale",0],["use random different-sized cubes",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagmass","caption":"Flagship lab: tip a balance, add cubes until level, record number+unit, reject bigger=heavier, read a scale, then challenge.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_4m8BimI4G5":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"4:23 means…","choices":["23 minutes past 4","23 minutes to 4","quarter to 5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Before :30, use minutes past.","explain":"4:23 is 23 minutes past 4."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Digital 2:09 matches…","choices":["9 minutes past 2","9 minutes to 2","half past 2"],"answer":0,"hint":":09 is still early in the hour.","explain":"2:09 is nine minutes past 2."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which is the mix-up?","choices":["Every time must land on a big number","3:17 is 17 minutes past 3","5:42 is 18 minutes to 6"],"answer":0,"hint":"Small marks let you read every minute.","explain":"Times can land between big numbers; each mark is one minute."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Minute marks between numbers…","choices":["each mark is 1 minute","each mark is 5 minutes","only hours matter"],"answer":0,"hint":"Big jumps are 5 minutes; small marks are 1.","explain":"Each small mark between numbers is one minute."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"6:51 as minutes to…","choices":["9 minutes to 7","51 minutes to 7","9 minutes past 6"],"answer":0,"hint":"60 − 51 = 9, next hour is 7.","explain":"6:51 is 9 minutes to 7 because 60 − 51 = 9, and the next hour is 7."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Minutes showing {a}. Minutes to next hour?","answer":"60-a","hint":"Subtract the minute reading from 60 to find minutes left.","explain":"60 − {a} = {=60-a} minutes to the next hour.","params":{"a":[40,58]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Start at 10 minutes past. Add {a} minutes. New minute reading?","answer":"10+a","hint":"Add on from 10; if you pass 60, wrap.","explain":"10 + {a} = {=10+a} minutes past the hour.","params":{"a":[1,20]}},{"type":"order","id":"m8","prompt":"Order: set 3:17 on a clock.","items":["put the short hand near 3","move the long hand 17 marks","check digital 3:17"],"hint":"Hour hand near 3, minute hand on 17.","explain":"Set the hour area, count 17 minute marks, confirm digitally."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Three small marks past 12 means…","choices":["3 minutes past","15 minutes past","3 o'clock"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each small mark is one minute, not five.","explain":"Three single marks past 12 is 3 minutes past."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"3:17 in words is…","choices":["17 minutes past 3","17 minutes to 3","quarter past 3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Before half past, say minutes past.","explain":"3:17 is seventeen minutes past 3."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"5:42 as minutes to…","choices":["18 minutes to 6","42 minutes to 6","18 minutes past 5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Minutes to = 60 minus the minute reading.","explain":"60 − 42 = 18, so 5:42 is 18 minutes to 6."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Minutes showing {a}. How many minutes to the next hour?","answer":"60-a","hint":"Subtract the minute reading from 60.","explain":"60 − {a} = {=60-a} minutes to the next hour.","params":{"a":[35,55]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"Long hand on minute mark {a}. Minutes past?","answer":"a","hint":"Count one minute per small mark from 12.","explain":"Mark {a} past 12 means {a} minutes past.","params":{"a":[1,9]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Between the big numbers are small marks. Each small mark is one minute.","3:17 means 17 minutes past 3 — the long hand is not only on big numbers.","After half past you can say minutes to the next hour: 5:42 is 18 minutes to 6.","A digital clock and a clock face can show the same time in two ways."],"adult":["NC Y3 Measurement: tell and write the time from an analogue clock to the nearest minute; compare with digital. Builds on Y2 five-minute intervals (flagmins) and hours/half hours (flagclock).","Words: minute mark, minutes past, minutes to, digital, nearest minute. Common mistakes: reading only multiples of 5; swapping past and to; treating :09 as minutes to the hour.","In the lab your child counts seven marks to 3:07, sets 3:17 and 4:23, makes 5:42 as minutes to 6, matches 2:09, and spots the big-number-only mix-up."]},"familyTip":"Point to a wall clock and ask for the exact minute, not only 'about quarter past'. Say a digital time like 4:23 and have your child set the long hand.","keyIdea":"Every small mark on the clock is one minute. Read times like 3:17 and 5:42 — minutes past before half past, and minutes to the next hour after half past — and match the digital form.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Minute-time idea or mix-up? Drag each clue, or tap it then a box.","opts":{"bins":["True minute idea","Myth / mix-up"],"items":[["each small mark is 1 minute",0],["3:17 is 17 minutes past 3",0],["every time must land on a big number",1],["5:42 is 18 minutes to 6",0],["2:09 is 9 minutes to 2",1],["digital and analogue show the same time",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtomin","caption":"Flagship lab: count minute marks, set 3:17 and 4:23, read minutes to (5:42), match digital 2:09, catch multiples-of-5-only myth.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_DOe893F6gN":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Unit for how hot the day is?","choices":["°C","cm","litres"],"answer":0,"hint":"Heat and cold need a temperature unit.","explain":"Temperature uses degrees Celsius (°C)."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which is the mix-up?","choices":["Always use cm for every length","Use m for a playground","Use ml for a medicine spoon"],"answer":0,"hint":"Long distances need larger length units.","explain":"Not every length should use cm — playgrounds need metres."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Juice carton capacity uses…","choices":["ml or litres","°C","kg only"],"answer":0,"hint":"A carton holds liquid — think capacity, not heat or mass.","explain":"Capacity of a carton is in ml or litres."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Paper width best unit?","choices":["cm","km","°C"],"answer":0,"hint":"A sheet of paper is short across.","explain":"Paper width is a small length — centimetres."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A grape’s mass is best in…","choices":["g","kg","m"],"answer":0,"hint":"A grape is light; pick the small mass unit, not kilograms.","explain":"A grape is about grams, not kilograms or metres."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"How many cm in {a} metres?","answer":"a*100","hint":"1 m = 100 cm. Multiply metres by 100.","explain":"{a} metres equals {=a*100} centimetres.","params":{"a":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"{a} litres as 100 ml cups (10 per litre)?","answer":"a*10","hint":"Multiply litres by 10 for 100 ml cups.","explain":"{a} litres is about {=a*10} cups of 100 ml.","params":{"a":[2,5]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Choosing units means…","choices":["matching unit to length, mass, heat or capacity","always using cm","using kg for temperature"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think which attribute you are measuring.","explain":"Pick m/cm, kg/g, °C, or ml/litres to match the job."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Best unit for a pencil’s length?","choices":["cm","m","kg"],"answer":0,"hint":"A pencil is short — think small length units.","explain":"A pencil is measured in centimetres, not metres or kilograms."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Silly unit for a swimming pool’s capacity?","choices":["millilitres (ml)","metres for length","large capacity units"],"answer":0,"hint":"Millilitres are tiny drops.","explain":"Millilitres are far too small for a pool’s capacity."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Best unit for a full school bag’s mass?","choices":["kg","g","°C"],"answer":0,"hint":"Heavy to lift — grams or kilograms?","explain":"A full bag is about kilograms, not grams or degrees."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"How many centimetres are in {a} metres?","answer":"a*100","hint":"1 metre = 100 centimetres. Multiply the metres by 100.","explain":"{a} metres equals {=a*100} centimetres — useful when you switch length units.","params":{"a":[2,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"A jug holds {a} litres. About how many 100 ml cups is that (10 cups per litre × litres)?","answer":"a*10","hint":"Each litre is about ten 100 ml cups.","explain":"{a} litres × 10 cups per litre ≈ {=a*10} cups.","params":{"a":[1,4]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Different jobs need different units.","Length uses centimetres or metres. Mass uses grams or kilograms.","Temperature uses degrees Celsius. Capacity uses millilitres or litres.","A tiny unit for a huge thing — or the wrong kind of unit — is a mix-up."],"adult":["NC Y2 Measurement: choose and use the right units — m or cm for length, kg or g for mass, °C for temperature, litres or ml for capacity. Distinct from Measuring Length and Height, Measuring mass and weight, and reading a thermometer.","Words: unit, centimetre, metre, gram, kilogram, Celsius, millilitre, litre. Common mistakes: always using cm; using kg for heat; using ml for a swimming pool.","In the lab your child matches object to unit, spots a silly unit, estimates then picks, chooses °C, catches the always-cm myth, and sorts capacity units."]},"familyTip":"Point at a pencil, a door, a juice carton and a bag. Ask: cm or m? ml or litres? g or kg?","keyIdea":"Pick the unit that fits what you measure: cm or m for length, g or kg for mass, °C for temperature, ml or litres for capacity — not one unit for everything.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Sensible unit idea or mix-up? Drag each clue, or tap it then a box.","opts":{"bins":["True unit idea","Myth / mix-up"],"items":[["pencil length → cm",0],["playground length → m",0],["always use cm for every length",1],["day’s heat → °C",0],["full school bag → kg",0],["swimming pool → millilitres",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagunits","caption":"Flagship lab: match pencil→cm, spot silly pool units, estimate bag→kg, pick °C, catch always-cm myth, sort capacity units.","opts":{"step":0}}}});
